.TH TKILL 2
.SH NAME
tkill \- send a signal to a thread of this process (SYS_TKILL)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

syscall4(SYS_TKILL, tid, sig, 0, 0);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SYS_TKILL
sends signal
.I sig
to the calling process as
.BR kill (2)
does, but a caught signal goes to thread
.I tid
of it, which runs the function, instead of the thread that set it. A
signal the process neither catches nor ignores ends it, as usual.
.PP
With
.BR sigwait (2)
in a signal's function, this is how one thread suspends another and
resumes it later (as WebKit's garbage collector does). The POSIX layer
uses it for
.BR pthread_kill .
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I sig
is 0 or out of range, or
.I tid
is not positive.
.TP
.B ESRCH
No thread
.I tid
in this process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kill (2),
.BR signal (2),
.BR sigwait (2)
